  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1105 PM CDT Sat Aug 29 2026\n\nTonight and Sunday:\n\nWater vapor imagery this evening features a prominent mid/upper\nlow pushing through the Pacific Northwest, with a broad belt of\nascent to its southeast pouring over the norther half of the \nCONUS. Zooming in locally, a messy surface pattern is in place \ndue to that belt of ascent kicking off pockets of showers and \nstorms from the High Plains, aiming their remnants towards \neastern Nebraska and Iowa. While those decaying showers \napproach, they will be joined by additional elevated convection\nthat is already starting develop across far southwestern \nNebraska before moving through eastern Nebraska during the \novernight hours. 00z soundings from both LBF and OAX show \nnotable dry layers in the lowest 300 mb, going alongside modest \nMUCAPE values to sustain storms with a low-end potential for \ndamaging wind gusts taking advantage of the strong low-level \nflow overnight. Latest runs of the short-term models have those\nshowers and storms entering the forecast area from the west at \n2 AM, moving out of the area to the east around 8 AM. \n\nAfter some isolated showers stunted afternoon warming today, \ntemperatures quickly rebounded and topped out in the upper 80s to \n90s across the area, working with uncomfortable dewpoints in the \nupper 60s to mid 70s. A repeat of this should happen Sunday, where \nmorning showers and storms stunt initial warming while steady \nsouthwesterly winds help temps climb into the 90s area wide. Heat \nindices are forecast to top out in the 100-104 range across \nsoutheast Nebraska and southwest Iowa, where lingering high \ndewpoints will exist into the afternoon hours while areas north of \nthere see some dry air keep conditions more bearable for those \noutside. CAMs keep pockets of afternoon showers possible, and will \nbe the main failure mode for the hot forecast, though you will not \nfind a meteorologist around that wouldn't mind underachieving \ntemperatures in place of stifling heat.  Heading into the overnight \nhours, elevated convection with low-end downburst potential develops \nonce again on the nose of the low-level jet, mostly across southeast \nNebraska. \n\nMonday and Beyond:\n\nWarmer than normal temperatures continue to define the forecast \nthrough the work week as southwesterly mid/upper flow early turns \ninto a building ridge that takes over the central CONUS heading into \nthe weekend and early next week. Highs in the upper 80s to 90s will \ncontinue to hold on as cooler air is stuck well to the north, \nleaving many folks attending school-related outdoor activities \nand practices wishing for the fall weather to arrive. \n\nWith no strong cold fronts to clear out moisture across the \narea, severe storm potential exists Monday afternoon into the \novernight hours across Nebraska and Iowa where a weak boundary \nwill sit. Early peeks at the extended CAMs shows little in the \nform of afternoon storms, but that amount of moisture with the \nadded lift of an incoming shortwave from Colorado could be \nenough for a few to form or waft in from the west. The \nuncertainty for storms only increases Tuesday into the remainder\nof the week, where steep lapse rates remain close to the area, \njuxtaposed to increasing influence of ridging in the mid/upper \nlevels to reduce the ambient shear. We'll rely on pulse \nthunderstorms as our moisture for the area, with no particular \nday through the week showing widespread rainfall despite daily \nchances (though only 10-20%) for showers and storms.",
